# Golden vectors for the pcap worked example.
#
# Not laid out here. text2pcap wrote the capture from four packets of 1, 14,
# 60 and 300 random bytes, and editcap cut it to a 40-byte snapshot length,
# Wireshark 4.4.18 both:
#
#   text2pcap -q -F pcap packets.txt full.pcap
#   editcap -F pcap -s 40 full.pcap cut.pcap
#
# So the header's `snaplen` is 40, and the last two records keep 40 bytes of
# packets 60 and 300 long: `incl_len` below `orig_len`, which is the case a
# length read from the wrong field gets wrong. tshark reads the same four
# records back -- cap_len 1, 14, 40, 40 and len 1, 14, 60, 300.
#
# Little-endian, as the machine that wrote it is. A big-endian file is the
# same records byte-swapped, and no tool here writes one;
# test_a_struct_takes_its_byte_order.py builds both.

pcap snapshot_40   D4 C3 B2 A1 02 00 04 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 28 00 00 00 01 00 00 00 92 B2 F5 6A 01 00 00 00 01 00 00 00 01 00 00 00 82 92 B2 F5 6A 02 00 00 00 0E 00 00 00 0E 00 00 00 B7 0E EE 7F 1A 50 39 BE F0 7E C2 34 7F 06 92 B2 F5 6A 03 00 00 00 28 00 00 00 3C 00 00 00 6E D0 8F 5D C7 51 24 47 E3 40 43 00 02 6B 6E 54 55 94 A0 65 68 5D 64 C4 98 0B B8 D4 54 4A 87 21 A9 9A 01 AD 21 9E B5 9C 92 B2 F5 6A 04 00 00 00 28 00 00 00 2C 01 00 00 3C 7D EC B0 B5 80 EC 37 BC 97 12 DD 2E 6A AE B9 4B AE 8D 2F 9F A2 9C 5A 28 4C 9E F7 52 18 29 CF 10 79 B0 80 E9 D7 4A 1C
